<main>

<section class="hero">
  <div class="hero-text">
    <h1>Response curves and deadzones for joysticks, gamepads, and wheels on Linux</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      xmorpheo reshapes the axes of HID input devices in the kernel, with
      HID-BPF. Curves, deadzones, inverted, swapped, and combined axes apply
      to the raw input reports, before any driver or game reads them. So
      they work in every game and application, native or in Proton and
      Wine, without support from the game.
    </p>
    <p class="actions">
      <a class="button primary" href="docs/INSTALL.html">Install xmorpheo</a>
      <a class="button" href="docs/RECIPES.html">Browse the recipes</a>
    </p>
    <p class="release">@VERSION_LINE@</p>
  </div>
  <figure class="plot">
    <svg viewBox="-12 -12 224 224" role="img" aria-labelledby="plot-title">
      <title id="plot-title">An expo curve with k = 0.6 next to the unchanged straight line: flatter around the center, full deflection at the ends</title>
      <g class="grid">
        <path d="M10 55H190M10 145H190M55 10V190M145 10V190"/>
      </g>
      <g class="axes">
        <path d="M10 100H190M100 10V190"/>
      </g>
      <path class="identity" d="M10 190L190 10"/>
      <path class="curve" d="M10.0 190.0 L14.5 180.5 L19.0 171.8 L23.5 163.8 L28.0 156.4 L32.5 149.8 L37.0 143.7 L41.5 138.2 L46.0 133.3 L50.5 128.8 L55.0 124.8 L59.5 121.1 L64.0 117.9 L68.5 114.9 L73.0 112.3 L77.5 109.8 L82.0 107.6 L86.5 105.6 L91.0 103.7 L95.5 101.8 L100.0 100.0 L104.5 98.2 L109.0 96.3 L113.5 94.4 L118.0 92.4 L122.5 90.2 L127.0 87.7 L131.5 85.1 L136.0 82.1 L140.5 78.9 L145.0 75.2 L149.5 71.2 L154.0 66.7 L158.5 61.8 L163.0 56.3 L167.5 50.2 L172.0 43.6 L176.5 36.2 L181.0 28.2 L185.5 19.5 L190.0 10.0"/>
      <text x="186" y="114" text-anchor="end">stick</text>
      <text x="106" y="20">game</text>
    </svg>
    <figcaption>
      <code>curve = "(1 - k) * x + k * pow(x, 3)"</code>
      with <code>k = 0.6</code>: finer control around the center, full
      deflection at the ends.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<section class="path" aria-labelledby="path-title">
  <h2 id="path-title">One place for every game</h2>
  <ol class="flow">
    <li><strong>Your device</strong> sends HID reports.</li>
    <li><strong>xmorpheo</strong> rewrites the configured axes in the kernel.</li>
    <li><strong>Everything that reads the device</strong> gets the new values:
      evdev and SDL, drivers like
      <a href="https://github.com/atar-axis/xpadneo">xpadneo</a>, and
      games in Proton or Wine that read <code>hidraw</code>.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    The device keeps its vendor and product ID, so games that bind their
    controls to a specific device still recognize it. There is no virtual
    device and no daemon: once attached, the curves stay in the kernel until
    you detach them, and udev attaches them again on every replug.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="uses" aria-labelledby="uses-title">
  <h2 id="uses-title">What it does</h2>
  <dl>
    <div>
      <dt><a href="docs/RECIPES.html#finer-control-around-the-center">Response curves and expo</a></dt>
      <dd>Finer aiming with a flight stick or gamepad stick, with any
        formula, like the expo curves of RC transmitters.
        <a href="https://github.com/xpadneo/xmorpheo/blob/@REF@/examples/vkb-gladiator-evo.toml">Flight stick example</a>,
        <a href="https://github.com/xpadneo/xmorpheo/blob/@REF@/examples/xbox-wireless-controller.toml">gamepad example</a>.</dd>
    </div>
    <div>
      <dt><a href="docs/RECIPES.html#add-a-deadzone">Deadzones</a></dt>
      <dd>Around the center of a worn stick, or at the start of a pedal,
        with full deflection still reachable.</dd>
    </div>
    <div>
      <dt><a href="docs/RECIPES.html#reach-full-deflection-early">Full deflection before the end stop</a></dt>
      <dd>For sticks and pedals that never quite reach their maximum.</dd>
    </div>
    <div>
      <dt><a href="docs/RECIPES.html#invert-an-axis">Inverted axes</a></dt>
      <dd>For games that offer no invert option, or apply it to every
        device at once.</dd>
    </div>
    <div>
      <dt><a href="docs/RECIPES.html#swap-two-axes">Swapped axes and wheel pedals</a></dt>
      <dd>When a game mixes up brake and clutch, or for a left-handed
        gamepad layout.
        <a href="https://github.com/xpadneo/xmorpheo/blob/@REF@/examples/t300rs-swap-pedals.toml">Wheel pedal example</a>.</dd>
    </div>
    <div>
      <dt><a href="docs/RECIPES.html#combine-two-pedals-or-triggers-on-one-axis">Combined axes</a></dt>
      <dd>Gas and brake, or two triggers, on one axis for older games.
        <a href="https://github.com/xpadneo/xmorpheo/blob/@REF@/examples/xbox-combined-triggers.toml">Trigger example</a>.</dd>
    </div>
    <div>
      <dt><a href="docs/RECIPES.html#pin-an-axis-at-rest">Pinned axes</a></dt>
      <dd>Hold an axis still, like a twist that should not yaw.</dd>
    </div>
  </dl>
  <p>
    If you know curve tools like Joystick Gremlin from Windows: xmorpheo
    covers the axes on Linux, with curves and deadzones per axis, in a
    plain configuration file. Buttons, layers, and macros are
    <a href="docs/IDEAS.html">not done yet</a>.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="tested" aria-labelledby="tested-title">
  <h2 id="tested-title">Tested on real hardware</h2>
  <p>
    xmorpheo is not tied to a device or a game. It reads the report
    descriptor of any HID device and finds its axes. These devices are
    tested so far:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>VKB Gladiator EVO L and R, over USB.</li>
    <li>Xbox Wireless Controller (Series X|S) over Bluetooth LE with
      xpadneo.</li>
    <li>Thrustmaster T300 RS with hid-tmff2.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    xmorpheo is young: it works on these devices, but you may run into
    bugs, and the configuration format may still change before 1.0.
    <a href="https://github.com/xpadneo/xmorpheo/issues">Feedback and bug
    reports</a> are very welcome.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="start" aria-labelledby="start-title">
  <h2 id="start-title">Getting started</h2>
  <p>
    You need Linux 6.11 or newer with <code>CONFIG_HID_BPF</code> and BTF,
    and for building Rust, clang, bpftool, and the libelf and zlib
    development files.
  </p>
<pre><code>make
sudo make install
xmorpheo list
xmorpheo template 231d:0200 | sudo tee /etc/xmorpheo/conf.d/50-mystick.toml
xmorpheo attach --all --dry-run
sudo xmorpheo attach --all</code></pre>
  <p>
    Put the <code>VENDOR:PRODUCT</code> of your device from
    <code>xmorpheo list</code> in place of <code>231d:0200</code>, and give
    the axes in the template a curve.
    <a href="docs/INSTALL.html">Installing</a> and
    <a href="docs/CONFIG.html">the configuration</a> explain each step,
    <a href="docs/FORMULAS.html">Formulas</a> the curves.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="caution" aria-labelledby="caution-title">
  <h2 id="caution-title">Force feedback devices</h2>
  <p>
    On force feedback devices such as wheels and joysticks, a wrong
    configuration, like an inverted or swapped axis, can make the game
    drive the device with full force against its stops. Depending on the
    device, this can damage the hardware or hurt hands, fingers, or thumbs.
    Test new configurations with the force feedback of the game turned
    down or off, and keep your hands clear. You use xmorpheo at your own
    risk, it comes without any warranty, see
    <a href="https://github.com/xpadneo/xmorpheo/blob/@REF@/README.md#safety-and-liability">Safety and Liability</a>.
  </p>
</section>

